## 3. Measurement of RV–PA Coupling

### 3.1 Gold Standard (Invasive)

- **Right heart catheterization with pressure–volume (PV) loops**:
    - Ees derived from end-systolic pressure–volume relationship.
    - Ea = end-systolic pressure / stroke volume.
    - **Ees/Ea ratio**:
        - Normal: **1.5–2.0**
        - <1.0: severe uncoupling, high risk of RV failure. [^1_1]
- Use: research, advanced hemodynamic labs, selected complex ICU cases (e.g., severe PH, mechanical circulatory support decisions).


### 3.2 Bedside Echocardiographic Surrogates (ICU-relevant)

#### 3.2.1 TAPSE/PASP (most validated)

- **Formula**:

$$
TAPSE/PASP = {TAPSE (mm)}/{PASP (mmHg)}
$$
- **PASP estimation**:
    - PASP ≈ 4 × (TR peak velocity)² + RAP (if TR present).
- **Thresholds** (commonly used):
    - **>0.55 mm/mmHg**: preserved coupling.
    - **0.36–0.55**: borderline.
    - **<0.36 mm/mmHg**: uncoupling; poor prognosis.
    - Some studies use **<0.45** or **<0.33** as high-risk cut-offs. [^1_7][^1_4][^1_5][^1_8]
- **Advantages**:
    - Simple, quick, widely available.
    - Better prognostic performance than TAPSE alone in PH, HF, sepsis, cardiac ICU. [^1_4][^1_5][^1_2]
- **Limitations**:
    - TAPSE is angle-dependent and reflects mainly longitudinal function.
    - PASP estimation requires adequate TR jet; RAP estimation introduces error.
    - Load-dependent, though less so than TAPSE alone.


#### 3.2.2 TAPSE/TRV (simplified, no RAP needed)

- **Formula**:

$$
\text{TAPSE/TRV} = \frac{\text{TAPSE (mm)}}{\text{TR peak velocity (m/s)}}
$$
- Avoids RAP estimation; useful when TR jet is present but RAP uncertain.
- Lower values indicate worse coupling; specific cutoffs less standardized than TAPSE/PASP but trend is similar. [^1_1][^1_9]


#### 3.2.3 TASV/RVSP (3D or biplane)

- **TASV**: tricuspid annular systolic velocity (from TDI).
- **RVSP**: RV systolic pressure (≈ PASP if no RVOT obstruction).
- Low TASV/RVSP associated with higher short- and long-term mortality in cardiac ICU. [^1_3]


#### 3.2.4 Strain-based coupling: RVGLS or RVFWLS / PASP

- **RV global longitudinal strain (RVGLS)** or **RV free-wall LS (RVFWLS)** divided by PASP.
- More sensitive to subtle RV dysfunction; adds incremental prognostic value over TAPSE/PASP in some cohorts (e.g., secondary TR, HF). [^1_10][^1_11]
- Requires good image quality and strain software; less routine in all ICUs.


#### 3.2.5 FAC/PASP (fractional area change)

- **FAC** = (RV end-diastolic area − end-systolic area) / end-diastolic area.
- FAC/PASP ratio has been studied but generally performs similarly or slightly worse than TAPSE/PASP for prognosis. [^1_12]

